{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0061","statement":"The proposed system achieves 76.63% (macro-averaged F-score), 1.4% higher than the top system in the shared task.","source_span":"state-of-the-art shared task performance of $76.63\\%$, $1.4\\%$ higher than the top system"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0061","statement":"The best score in the tweetLID competition (top submission) was 75.2%.","source_span":"higher than the best score in the competition: 75.2%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0061","statement":"The tweetLID shared task requires identifying tweets in six languages: Spanish (es), Portuguese (pt), Catalan (ca), English (en), Galician (gl) and Basque (eu).","source_span":"identify the language of tweets written in Spanish (es), Portuguese (pt), Catalan (ca), English (en), Galician (gl) and Basque (eu)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0061","statement":"Adding the social model almost doubles the F-score for undecided (und) language tweets, from 18.85% to 34.95%.","source_span":"almost doubles the F-score for undecided (und) languages, either not in the set above or hard-to-identify, from 18.85% to 34.95%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0061","statement":"Using the content model alone is more effective for languages that are distinct in the set, namely English and Basque.","source_span":"using the content model alone is more effective for languages that are distinct in our set of languages (i.e. English and Basque)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0061","statement":"In the social graph, tweet-author (T-U) edges are weighted 100, user-follows-user (U-U) edges are weighted 1, and user-world (U-W) edges are weighted 0.001.","source_span":"T-U weighted 100 between a tweet and its author, U-U weighted 1 between two users in a \"follows\" relationship and U-W weighted 0.001 to ensure a connected graph"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0061","statement":"The content model is a 1 vs. all L2-regularised logistic regression model using character 2- to 5-gram features that do not span word boundaries.","source_span":"a 1 vs. all INLINEFORM0 regularised logistic regression model with character 2- to 5-grams features, not spanning over word boundaries"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0061","statement":"The evaluation corpus was manually labelled with six languages and evenly split into training and test collections.","source_span":"the supplied evaluation corpus, which has been manually labelled with six languages and evenly split into training and test collections"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0061","statement":"The social graph is composed of three types of nodes: tweets (T), users (U), and the \"world\" (W).","source_span":"composed of three types of nodes: tweets (T), users (U) and the \u201cworld\u201d (W)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0061","statement":"The system uses Junto's Modified Adsorption (mad) algorithm to propagate labels from labelled to unlabelled nodes.","source_span":"We use Junto (mad) BIBREF5 to propagate labels from labelled to unlabelled nodes"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0061","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What F-score did the proposed language identification system achieve in the tweetLID shared task, and how did it compare to the top submission?","answer":"It achieved a macro-averaged F-score of 76.63%, which is 1.4% higher than the top submission's score of 75.2%."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0061","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Which six languages did the tweetLID shared task require systems to identify?","answer":"Spanish (es), Portuguese (pt), Catalan (ca), English (en), Galician (gl), and Basque (eu)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0061","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How much did adding the social model improve the F-score for undecided (\"und\") language tweets?","answer":"It almost doubled the F-score for undecided languages, raising it from 18.85% to 34.95%."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0061","fact_refs":[4],"question":"For which languages did the content model alone perform better than combining it with the social model?","answer":"English and Basque, since they are distinct from the other languages in the set."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0061","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What weights are assigned to the different edge types in the social graph used for label propagation?","answer":"Tweet-author (T-U) edges are weighted 100, user-follows-user (U-U) edges are weighted 1, and user-world (U-W) edges are weighted 0.001 to keep the graph connected."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0061","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What type of content model does the system use, and what features does it rely on?","answer":"A 1 vs. all L2-regularised logistic regression model using character 2- to 5-gram features that do not span word boundaries."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0062","statement":"The Stanford Natural Language Inference (SNLI) Corpus contains over half a million examples.","source_span":"a NLI dataset that contains over a half a million examples"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0062","statement":"The accuracy of a classifier trained on the best generative model's dataset is only 2.7% lower than the accuracy of a classifier trained on the original, human-crafted dataset.","source_span":"The accuracy obtained by our best generative model is only 2.7% lower than the accuracy of the classifier trained on the original, human crafted dataset"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0062","statement":"Combining the best generated dataset with the original dataset achieves the highest classification accuracy.","source_span":"the best generated dataset combined with the original dataset achieves the highest accuracy"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0062","statement":"The flagship generative network, EmbedDecoder, consists only of a decoder, with the latent representation learned as an embedding for each training example separately.","source_span":"EmbedDecoder consists only of the decoder, and the latent representation is learned as an embedding for each training example separately"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0062","statement":"Natural Language Inference classifies a hypothesis sentence relative to a premise as entailment, contradiction, or neutral.","source_span":"correctly decide whether a sentence (referred to as a premise) entails or contradicts or is neutral in respect to another sentence (a hypothesis)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0062","statement":"Datasets that obtain higher ROUGE or METEOR scores do not necessarily yield higher classification accuracies.","source_span":"datasets that obtain higher ROUGE or METEOR scores do not necessarily yield higher classification accuracies"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0062","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How many examples does the SNLI corpus contain?","answer":"Over half a million examples."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0062","fact_refs":[1],"question":"How does the classification accuracy from the best generative model's dataset compare to that of the original SNLI dataset?","answer":"It is only 2.7% lower than the accuracy of the classifier trained on the original, human-crafted dataset."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0062","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Which combination of training data produces the highest classification accuracy in this study?","answer":"Combining the best generated dataset with the original dataset achieves the highest accuracy."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0062","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What makes the EmbedDecoder generative model architecturally different from a standard encoder-decoder network?","answer":"EmbedDecoder has only a decoder — instead of an encoder, the latent representation is learned as a separate embedding for each training example."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0062","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are the three possible relationships a hypothesis can have with a premise in Natural Language Inference?","answer":"Entailment, contradiction, or neutral."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0062","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Do higher ROUGE or METEOR scores on a generated dataset guarantee higher classification accuracy?","answer":"No, the paper finds that datasets with higher ROUGE or METEOR scores do not necessarily yield higher classification accuracies."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0063","statement":"The WebQA dataset contains more than 42,000 questions and 556,000 evidences.","source_span":"a large scale human annotated real-world QA dataset WebQA with more than 42k questions and 556k evidences"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0063","statement":"The proposed sequence labeling model achieves an F1 score of 74.69% on WebQA with word-based input.","source_span":"our model outperforms the baselines significantly with an F1 score of 74.69% with word-based input"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0063","statement":"Performance drops only 3.72 F1 points when using character-based input instead of word-based input.","source_span":"the performance drops only 3.72 F1 points with more challenging character-based input"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0063","statement":"The model achieves an F1 score of 70.97% on character-based input.","source_span":"our model even achieves an F1 score of 70.97% on character-based input, which is comparable with the 74.69% F1 score on word-based input"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0063","statement":"The model casts QA as a sequence labeling problem, using a CRF to label each word in the evidence as beginning (B), inside (I), or outside (O) of the answer.","source_span":"we use CRF BIBREF17 to assign a label to each word in the evidence to indicate whether the word is at the beginning (B), inside (I) or outside (O) of the answer"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0063","statement":"The model architecture consists of three components: a question LSTM, three-layer evidence LSTMs, and a CRF layer.","source_span":"The model consists of three components: (1) question LSTM for computing question representation; (2) evidence LSTMs for evidence analysis; and (3) a CRF layer for sequence labeling"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0063","statement":"The question LSTM is a single-layer LSTM equipped with a single time attention.","source_span":"The question LSTM in a form of a single layer LSTM equipped with a single time attention"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0063","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How many questions and evidences does the WebQA dataset contain?","answer":"WebQA contains more than 42,000 questions and 556,000 evidences."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0063","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What F1 score does the proposed model achieve on WebQA using word-based input?","answer":"74.69%."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0063","fact_refs":[2,3],"question":"How much does performance drop when the model uses character-based input instead of word-based input, and what F1 score does it reach?","answer":"Performance drops only 3.72 F1 points, giving an F1 score of 70.97% with character-based input, compared to 74.69% with word-based input."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0063","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How does the model indicate which words in the evidence form the answer?","answer":"It uses a CRF layer to tag each word in the evidence as beginning (B), inside (I), or outside (O) of the answer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0063","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What are the three main components of the proposed neural QA sequence labeling model?","answer":"A question LSTM for computing the question representation, three-layer evidence LSTMs for evidence analysis, and a CRF layer for sequence labeling."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0063","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What kind of LSTM is used to compute the question representation?","answer":"A single-layer LSTM equipped with a single time attention."}
